---
title: Şehriyeli Pilav
description: Turkish basmati pilav with toasted vermicelli, browned onion, cumin and a little tomato paste, cooked in vegetable stock and finished with toasted nuts.
author: Invented by Claude
course: side
cuisine: turkish
diet: vegetarian
difficulty: easy
servings: 4
time: 1h10m
prep time: 30m
cook time: 40m
tags: [rice, pasta, one-pot]
---

Put @basmati rice{300%g} in a #bowl, cover with warm water and add @salt{=1%tsp}. Soak ~rice{30%minutes}, then drain and rinse in a #sieve until the water runs clear. Soaking and rinsing removes surface starch, which is what keeps the grains separate.

> No time to soak: rinse the rice in several changes of water until clear and use 600 ml stock instead of 500 ml.

Meanwhile, toast @flaked almonds{40%g}(or pine nuts) in a dry #heavy-based pot{} over medium heat, stirring, ~{3%minutes} until golden. Tip out onto a plate; nuts go from golden to burnt quickly.

Bring @vegetable stock{500%ml} to a simmer in a #saucepan and keep it hot.

In the same pot, melt @butter{30%g} with @olive oil{1%tbsp} over medium heat. Add @onion{1}(finely diced) and cook ~{8%minutes}, stirring now and then, until soft and deep golden.

Add @vermicelli{50%g}(broken into 2 cm pieces) and stir constantly ~{3%minutes} until the pasta is an even nutty brown. Watch it closely at the end; it darkens fast.

Stir in @tomato paste{1%tbsp}, @ground cumin{1%tsp} and @black pepper{0.5%tsp} and cook ~{1%minutes} until the paste darkens and smells sweet rather than raw.

Add the drained rice and stir ~{2%minutes} until every grain is coated in fat and looks slightly translucent at the edges.

Pour in the hot stock and add @salt{0.5%tsp}, less if your stock is salty. Bring to a boil, then cover tightly, turn the heat to the lowest setting and cook ~{15%minutes} without lifting the lid.

Take off the heat, lift the lid, lay a #clean tea towel{} over the pot and put the lid back on. Rest ~{10%minutes}: the towel absorbs steam so the rice turns fluffy, not wet.

Fluff with a #fork, check the salt and scatter the toasted nuts on top.

> Orzo instead of vermicelli: use 50 g orzo and toast it ~5 minutes, as it is thicker and takes longer to colour.

> Keeps three days in the fridge. Reheat with a splash of water under a lid.
